Performance Analytics Tool gives insights on delphix CDE performance.

It can be accessed through the Delphix Engine UI, as well as the CLI and through the web services API. 

accessed by clicking the Resources tab located on the Delphix Continuous Data Engine banner, and selecting Performance Analytics.

The PAT provides capabilities that can help us narrow down the cause of observed performance 
those tools operate in 2 central concepts:
    *statistic
    *statistic slices

The Delphix Engine ships with a default set of collected statistics:

    *CPU Utilization : Total CPU utilization for all CPUs. This statistic includes both kernel and user time.

    *Network Throughput: Measures throughput in bytes and packets, broken down by sent vs. received data and by the network interface. Each network interface shows four graphed lines: bytes sent, bytes received, packets sent, and packets received.

    *Disk I/O : Measures a number of I/O operations, and the latencies and throughput of the underlying storage layer. The statistic is represented by the graphs - a column chart for I/O operations, a heat map for latency distribution, and a line chart for throughput.

    *NFS(Network File System) : Measures I/O operations and the latency /throughput of the NFS server layer on the delphix engine. This statistic is useful for diagnosing  performance of dSources and VDBs using NFS mounts (for example, Oracle and PostgreSQL).

    *iSCSI(Internet small computer system interface):
    it measure I/O operation andthe latency andthe throughput ofthe iSCSI server layer in the delphix engine


The actual data a statistic can collect is described through a set of axes, each of which describes one "dimension" of that statistic. For example, the statistic associated with Network File System (NFS) operations has a latency axis, as well as an operation type axis (among many others), which allows users to see NFS latencies split by whether they were reads or writes.

each axis has some important info embedded in it:
    * The Name
    * The Value Type(A value type, which tells you what kind of data will be collected for this axis. )
    * The Constraint Type(A constraint type is only relevant while creating a statistic slice.)

However, if you had collected the operation type axis as well as the latency axis, you would get two streams of datapoints: one for read operations, and one for write operations.

• Latency Axis (The Measurement): This simply measures how long an operation took in time (microseconds or milliseconds), regardless of what kind of action it was.
• Operation Type Axis (The Filter): This labels what the operation actually was (Read vs. Write).

so , what is operation type axis and latency type axis in statistics of performance analytics tool??

    Operation type and latency axes work together to help administrators pinpoint exact performance bottlenecks across storage, network, and database I/O

And these lead to 2 data stream , one for read operations and one fro write , how and why??

    When you slice latency by the operation type axis in the Delphix Performance Analytics Tool, the chart splits into two primary data streams: Read operations and Write operations.

    • The read data stream: tracks requests where a target host (a Virtual Database) is reading existing data blocks from the Delphix Engine over NFS or iSCSI.

    • The write data stream: racks requests where a target host is writing new or modified data blocks back to the Delphix Engine

Statistic "Slices":

    "Slices" are so named because each one provides a subset of the information available from the parent statistic it is associated with.
    
Statistics defines what data could be collected and are auto populated by the system

But, Statistic slices are what actually collect the data, and users create slices when specific performance data needs to be gathered.

 Each slice is an instantiation of exactly one statistic, and can only gather data that is described by that statistic.

checklist for instantiation of slices under a specific statistic description:
    # The statistic type this slice is associated with. This must be the same type as the statistic of which this is an instantiation.
    # The collection interval, which must be greater than the minimum collection interval the parent statistic gives.
    # The axes of the parent statistic this slice will collect.

WORKING WITH PERFORMANCE ANALYTICS GRAPHS:
    for each set of collected statistics there exists a specific graph like for CPU, Network throughput, disk i/o , NFS and iSCSI

    Tabs ib Graph: Timescale, chart tab, timeline sector
    for network chart - network interface, graph legend, pause/play button

        Left/Right Button: The Left and Right Buttons allow you to Scroll the Available Data Timeline by a specified time range depending on the current Zoom Level. 

        Graph Value Tooltip: The graphs can show you the value, along with the time stamp, for a specific data point across the timeline

        Latency Range Selector : The Latency Range Selector controls the lower and upper limits for displayed latency buckets. You can drag the lower and upper controls to drill down into a specific range of latency buckets. 

        Latency Outlier Selector:The Latency outliers feature filters out infrequent latencies (outliers) based on a percentage threshold. Its range is 0%-10%, with a default of 0% and can be adjusted by sliding the bar along the percentage line.

what is Latency? Latency is simply delay—the exact amount of time it takes for a request to travel from Point A to Point B and get a response back.

